<p>
    DevFest Berlin is a community event for everyone who builds with technology. We want every attendee,
    speaker, sponsor, volunteer and organizer to have a good day, free from harassment and discrimination.
    Everyone who takes part agrees to this Code of Conduct.
</p>

<h2>Our standards</h2>
<p>
    DevFest Berlin follows the
    <a href="https://www.google.com/events/policy/anti-harassmentpolicy.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Event Community Guidelines and Anti-Harassment Policy</a>
    and the <a href="https://support.google.com/developergroups/answer/3340512" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Developer Groups event Code of Conduct</a>.
    In short:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Be excellent to each other.</strong> Treat everyone with respect, whatever their background, experience or opinions.</li>
    <li><strong>No harassment.</strong> This includes offensive comments about gender, gender identity and expression, age, sexual orientation, disability, physical appearance, body size, race, ethnicity, religion or technology choices.</li>
    <li><strong>No unwelcome attention.</strong> No intimidation, stalking or following, unwanted photography or recording, inappropriate physical contact, or unwelcome sexual attention.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep content appropriate.</strong> No sexualized images or language in talks, slides, booths or online. This applies to speakers and sponsors too.</li>
    <li><strong>Speak up.</strong> If you see or hear something, check on the person and tell a member of the organizing team.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    The Code of Conduct applies everywhere the event takes place: talks, workshops, codelabs, the expo area,
    social events, hallway conversations, social media and online channels.
</p>

<h2>How to report</h2>
<p>
    If you are being harassed, notice that someone else is, or have any other concern, please contact us
    right away. You can talk to us in confidence, and you do not have to be sure that something breaks the rules.
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>On the day:</strong> find any organizer or volunteer. You will recognize them by their organizer and volunteer badges.</li>
    <li><strong>Before or after the event, or online:</strong> email {{ contactLink(site.contactEmail) }}.</li>
    <li><strong>In an emergency:</strong> call 112 (ambulance and fire) or 110 (police).</li>
</ul>
<p>
    We can help you contact venue security or the police, walk you to a safe place, and do what we can to keep
    you safe for the rest of the event.
</p>

<h2>What happens next</h2>
<p>
    We look into every report. Details are shared only with the people who need them to act, which can include
    venue security or Google's community team (see <a href="/datenschutz/#code-of-conduct-reports">how we handle reports</a>).
    The organizers may take any action they consider appropriate, including a warning, removing a talk or booth,
    or asking the person to leave the event without a refund. Serious cases can lead to exclusion from future
    Google Developer Groups events.
</p>
